    "reply": "I added `tests/test_payments.py` with 14 standard-library `unittest` tests covering `line_total`, `order_total`, and `refund`. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` passes, and I did not touch anything under `shop/`.\n\nThe tests cover:\n- **`line_total`**: multiplication, zero quantity, and float prices.\n- **`order_total`**: empty order, single and multiple lines, tax applied at `TAX_RATE`, a known value (20 \u00d7 1.0825 \u2192 21.65), rounding to cents, and zero-quantity lines.\n- **`refund`**: subtraction, full refund, zero refund, and a float result.\n\nI found no bugs, so nothing is marked `expectedFailure`. One gap I did not test: `refund` does not check that the amount is within the order total, so a refund larger than the total returns a negative number. Whether that should raise an error is a product decision, so I left it alone. Tell me if you want a test that pins down the expected behavior.\n\nThe `tests/` directory did not exist before, so this is the first file in it. I did not add an `__init__.py`; discovery works without one.",
